---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Field from '../components/Field.astro';

// One real session on the acceptance application the hale repository
// ships, with the editor on scripted models; the same commands and the
// same record with a hosted model.
const session = `$ hale dna init .            # attach it: three files you own, rules the compiler enforces
$ hale dna run               # your program, with the organism inside it
$ hale dna ask document the chat server in main.hl
task t1 born for intent i1a08c0786c5 [pending]

$ hale dna review m1
review m1 [pending]: apply m1 (application): document the chat server in main.hl?
  needs maintainer · candidate dbbb49f550f3f021f390710803e48f1b3d1593ff

source diff (git 4319af28e30e .. dbbb49f550f3):
  +// documented by the organism: the rooms are the only way to the signer

semantic diff (hale model diff, baseline .. candidate):
  classification: source-only  ·  no semantic differences

evidence (fmt=0 check=0 verify=0 test=0 diff=0 rollback=0):
  check      yes    0     e3b0c44298fc
  test       yes    0     184a3407ce31
  rollback   yes    0     a06effb044d9

$ hale dna review m1 approve --as riley
review m1 settled: approve by riley
hale dna run: organism restarted (pid 1694392) as 8517c3db7499d3b3 build b1ac50c8c3ef
hale dna run: m1 observed healthy for 5s as 8517c3db7499d3b3`;
---
<Base title="DNA: your program, changing under review">
  <Field kind="tooling" />
  <section class="container prose">
    <span class="tag">/dna</span>
    <h1>Your program, changing under review.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Code is now written faster than anyone can read it. <strong>DNA</strong>
      is what a Hale application carries so that a change proposed by a model
      is made in a sandbox, verified with receipts, explained in the program's
      own terms, approved by a person, applied as a commit, restarted, watched,
      and undone if it fails — and so that what the model may touch is a rule
      the compiler enforces, not a line in a prompt.
    </p>

    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>
      An application with DNA has a change process built into it. You ask for
      something in a sentence. The organism makes a copy of the repository,
      lets a model edit one file under a fixed grant — read, edit, format,
      check — and commits the result in the copy. The toolchain then checks,
      verifies and tests that candidate, diffs it against the program's
      structure, rehearses rolling it back, and keeps every output under its
      own hash. You get one review: the source diff, the structural diff and
      the evidence, pinned to one commit. Your <em>approve</em> is the apply.
      The program rebuilds and restarts itself, is watched for a window, and
      the change is kept or rolled back. All of it is written to a
      hash-chained record you can walk afterwards.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three words carry the design. The <strong>genome</strong> is the source,
      the rules, and the assembly that says which models and gateways exist.
      The <strong>expression</strong> is the running process, with a model
      hash the observer can join to its instances. The
      {' '}<strong>experience</strong> is the record of what happened. When two of
      them disagree, the record is the authority.
    </p>

    <h2>Why, in 2026</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>More changes than you can read.</strong> Agents produce pull
      requests faster than reviewers can absorb them, so review degrades to
      skimming and CI becomes the only real check. DNA does not make reading
      unnecessary; it makes each change one thing, already verified, and
      explains it twice: the source diff, and what changed in the program's
      shape — who talks to whom, which effects are reached, whether a rule
      went from holding to violated. A rule that flips is one line in that
      view and a page of archaeology in the source.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Autonomy as a checkbox.</strong> Today an agent's permissions are
      a toggle in a tool: edit files, run commands, auto-approve. DNA writes
      the boundary into the program's own constitution — the editing step
      cannot reach git, the network, a deployment or the organism's memory —
      and <code>hale check</code> holds that against the wiring you actually
      built. Hand the editor a git handle and the build fails, with the path
      that shows why. Widening the boundary is a reviewed commit, like any
      other change.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Agents edit repositories; programs run.</strong> A change is
      usually judged by tests, in a checkout, with no idea what the program is
      doing in production. Hale programs are modeled by their compiler, so a
      candidate is measured on what it does to that model — an added component
      that reaches an outbound effect crosses a hard boundary before a human
      sees a line — and the running program carries its model hash, so the
      observer can say which instances express which version.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Provenance is a chat log.</strong> Why a change happened is
      reconstructed from PR descriptions and transcripts. DNA's record has the
      ask, the sandbox, every model call with its cost and digests (never the
      prompt), each check with its receipt, the verdict with a name on it,
      the apply, the restart, the outcome. <code>hale dna history m1</code> is
      one change's whole story.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Rollback is a hope.</strong> A change that breaks production is
      reverted by whoever notices. Here a change that crashes inside the
      observation window is reset to the commit it started from, mechanically,
      and the old program is started again; a change you reject after the
      fact goes the same way; a retried apply reads its own record and never
      commits twice.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      And it runs where your code is: in the <code>hale</code> binary, on your
      machine, with your model key or a local model. No control plane.
    </p>

    <h2>How</h2>
    <p>
      Attach it to a program that already passes its checks, start it, ask.
      This is a small chat server, with the editor on scripted models so the
      transcript is reproducible; a hosted model gives the same commands and
      the same record.
    </p>
    <div class="code"><div class="code-head"><span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span><span class="dot"></span><span class="code-title">terminal</span></div><pre><code>{session}</code></pre></div>
    <p>
      That is the whole surface: <code>init</code>, <code>run</code>,
      {' '}<code>ask</code>, <code>review</code>, <code>status</code>,
      {' '}<code>history</code>. The rest is how much autonomy you grant and
      which models you point it at — two edits in a file you own.
    </p>

    <h2>What it doesn't claim</h2>
    <p>
      Every change in this phase blocks on a person; post-review autonomy
      exists for refactors you opt into, and a rejection still rolls back. A
      restart is a restart, not a live state migration. And the structural
      view is blind to what a handler computes — a <code>+1</code> becoming
      {' '}<code>+100</code> is invisible to it — which is why the source diff is
      never optional. The design was gated on a test of exactly that: reviewers
      deciding from the structural diff alone, the source alone, and both. The
      combined view is what shipped, and the structural view is credited with
      what it earned: it decides shape and rules, and it says when it cannot
      decide.
    </p>

    <h2>Start</h2>
    <div class="install" style="margin-bottom:0.6rem;">
      <span class="sigil">$</span>
      <span class="cmd">hale dna init . &amp;&amp; hale dna run</span>
    </div>
    <p>
      The <a href="/docs/dna">user guide</a> is six short chapters: getting
      started, working with it, shaping it, what it will and won't do,
      troubleshooting. The deep dive — the record, the gateways, the review's
      pin, the autonomy rules — starts at
      {' '}<a href="/docs/dna/attach">how attaching works</a>. The design it
      implements is the
      {' '}<a href="https://github.com/hale-lang/hale/issues/521">Hale DNA RFC</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <style>
    .code pre { margin: 0; overflow-x: auto; }
    .code code { font-size: 0.86rem; line-height: 1.45; }
  </style>
</Base>
